-- title:
--   Adapted Lie basis with varpi e₀ = u e₁, uv = q
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and write $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2} =$ `Zp2 q` for the Witt vectors of $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$. Let $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ be a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain which is a $\mathbb{Z}_q$-algebra, adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $q$, that ideal being maximal, with algebraically closed residue field $k$; let $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ be a ring map and $X_0$ a special formal $O_D$-module of height $4$ over $k$ for the composite $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to k$ (a commutative $2$-dimensional formal group law with a $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$-action by law endomorphisms and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi = [q]$ and $\varpi\circ a = \sigma(a)\circ\varpi$, whose Lie module is the direct sum of the two invertible eigen-submodules `lieZero`, `lieOne`). Let $R$ be a Noetherian local $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra, complete for its maximal ideal, with a ring map $\mathrm{res}_R : R \to k$ compatible with the structure map, $\mathcal{X}$ a formal $O_D$-module over $R$ and $w_u$ an isomorphism $\mathcal{X}\bmod \mathrm{res}_R \xrightarrow{\sim} X_0$. Assume the pro-representability hypothesis: for every Artinian local $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra $A$ with surjective compatible $\mathrm{res}_A : A \to k$, every formal $O_D$-module $X$ over $A$ that is special for $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}\to A$ and of height $4$, and every isomorphism $w$ from $X\bmod\mathrm{res}_A$ to $X_0$, there is a unique $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-algebra map $\chi : R \to A$ with $\mathrm{res}_A\circ\chi = \mathrm{res}_R$ admitting an isomorphism $v : \mathcal{X}\otimes_\chi A \to X$ whose reduction followed by $w$ has the same series as $w_u$. Assume finally that the linear part of $\varpi$ on $X_0$ annihilates both `lieZero` and `lieOne` (the double-point condition). Then there exist $e_0 \in (\operatorname{Lie}\mathcal{X})_0$, $e_1 \in (\operatorname{Lie}\mathcal{X})_1$ — the eigen-submodules for the $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$-action through $O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$ — forming an $R$-basis of $\operatorname{Lie}\mathcal{X} = R^2$, in the sense that every element is uniquely $c_0 e_0 + c_1 e_1$, together with $u, v \in R$ such that the linear part of $\varpi$ on $\mathcal{X}$ sends $e_0 \mapsto u e_1$ and $e_1 \mapsto v e_0$, with $uv$ the image of $q$ in $R$ and both $u$ and $v$ in the maximal ideal of $R$.
--
--   This is Drinfeld's local computation at a double point: on the universal deformation of a special formal $O_D$-module whose $\varpi$ acts trivially on the Lie algebra, the uniformiser $\varpi$ is given in an adapted eigenbasis by a pair of parameters $u, v$ of the maximal ideal with $uv = q$. It feeds the identification of the deformation ring with the crossing model $O^{\mathrm{nr}}[[u,v]]/(uv-q)$ and the attendant statement on the maximal ideal, and is used in the rigidified comparison of deformations over dual numbers.
